    "field": "Hurricane climatology",
    "problem": "The cause of observed multidecadal variability in Atlantic major hurricane frequency is debated, with uncertainty over whether the driver is internal ocean dynamics, like the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), or external forcing from changes in anthropogenic aerosols.",
    "method": "Using observations and a coupled earth system model (GFDL-ESM2G), this study analyzes coherent multidecadal variations among Atlantic major hurricane frequency, the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC), and related climate indices to explain the recent decline in hurricane activity.",
    "task": "This paper investigates the cause of the recent decline in Atlantic major hurricane frequency (2005-2015), concluding it is associated with a weakening of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) rather than changes in anthropogenic sulfate aerosols.",
